{"abstract":"The Dagstuhl Seminar 22111 on \"Database indexing and query processing\", held from March 13 to March 18 2022, brought together researchers from academia and industry to discuss robustness in database management systems. This seminar was a continuation of previous seminars on the topic of Robust Query Processing, where we included indexing as a general topic and also discussed aspects that have not been addressed by the previous instances of the seminar. This article summarizes the main discussion topics, and presents the summary of the outputs of three work groups that discussed: i) storage architectures, ii) robust operators, and iii) indexing for data warehousing.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Borovica-Gajic, Renata","Graefe, Goetz","Lee, Allison W.","Sauer, Caetano","Tözün, Pinar"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384509"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","Germany","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Google Inc.","Salesforce","Snowflake","University of Melbourne"],"keyword":["Database","Execution","Hardware","Optimization","Performance","Query"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"database indexing and query processing dagstuhl seminar 000000022111","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/DagRep.12.3.82","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384509","ids":["itu-dca0d35d-b52f-4be7-8d4a-eddd8ff60017"],"keyword":["database","execution","hardware","optimization","performance","query"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-570292E72DE34E646C9440C283661F42"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/DagRep.12.3.82","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"au","key":"university of melbourne","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"University of Melbourne","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"google inc","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Google Inc.","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"snowflake","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Snowflake","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"de","key":"salesforce","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Salesforce","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Renata","key":"borovicagajicpau","last":"Borovica-Gajic","name":"Borovica-Gajic, Renata","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Goetz","key":"graefepau","last":"Graefe","name":"Graefe, Goetz","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Allison W.","key":"leepau","last":"Lee","name":"Lee, Allison W.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Caetano","key":"sauerpau","last":"Sauer","name":"Sauer, Caetano","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Pinar","key":"tözünpau","last":"Tözün","locid":"63342b24-28ec-40c5-a7d9-ec49867dc75d","name":"Tözün, Pinar","orcid":"0000-0001-6838-4854","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4230/dagrep.12.3.82","issn":["21925283"],"issue":"3","journal":"Dagstuhl Reports","pages":"82-96","publisher":"Schloss Dagstuhl - Leibniz-Zentrum fuer Informatik GmbH","status":"p","title":"Dagstuhl Reports","volume":"12"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-dca0d35d-b52f-4be7-8d4a-eddd8ff60017"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Database Indexing and Query Processing (Dagstuhl Seminar 22111)","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384509","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Since the publication of The Case for Learned Index Structures in 2018 [26], there has been a rise in research that focuses on learned indexes for different domains and with different functionalities. While the effectiveness of learned indexes as an alternative to traditional index structures such as B+Trees have already been demonstrated by several studies, previous work tend to focus on higher-level performance metrics such as throughput and index size. In this paper, our goal is to dig deeper and investigate how learned indexes behave at a micro-architectural level. More specifically, we focus on previously proposed learned index structure ALEX [10], which is a tree-based in-memory index structure that consists of a hierarchy of machine learned models. Unlike the original proposal for learned indexes, ALEX is designed from the ground up to allow updates and inserts. Therefore, it enables more dynamic workloads using learned indexes. In this work, we perform a micro-architectural analysis of ALEX and compare its behavior to the tree-based index structures that are not based on learned models, i.e., ART and B+Tree. Our results show that ALEX is bound by memory stalls, mainly stalls due to data misses from the last-level cache. Compared to ART and B+Tree, ALEX exhibits fewer stalls and a lower cycles-per-instruction value across different workloads. On the other hand, the amount of instructions required to handle out-of-bound inserts in ALEX can increase the instructions needed per request significantly (10X) for write-heavy workloads. However, the micro-architectural behavior shows that this increase in the instruction footprint exhibit high instruction-level parallelism, and, therefore, does not negatively impact the overall execution time.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Andersen, Mikkel Møller","Tözün, Pinar"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-692960"],"event":{"end":"2022-06-17","place":"Philadelphia, us","start":"2022-06-17","title":"5th International Workshop on Exploiting Artificial Intelligence Techniques for Data Management, aiDM 2022 - In conjunction with the 2022 ACM SIGMOD/PODS Conference","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2023-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"micro architectural analysis of a learned index","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://www.scopus.com/inward/record.url?scp=85137094838&partnerID=8YFLogxK","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-692960","identifier":[{"id":"85137094838","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-bb9a2673-86b5-4bbb-9f76-fcf7ebe27014"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-5-CD6F8117CF89B13CD41B4CBB1ACF987C"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Electronic Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mikkel Møller","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"6123a0f3-46da-430e-bb65-a35a8d00816f","name":"Andersen, Mikkel Møller","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Pinar","key":"tözünpau","last":"Tözün","name":"Tözün, Pinar","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3533702.3534917","eisbn":["9781450393775"],"pages":"1-12","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery (ACM)","series":"Proceedings of the 5th International Workshop on Exploiting Artificial Intelligence Techniques for Data Management, aiDM 2022 - In conjunction with the 2022 ACM SIGMOD/PODS Conference","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 5th International Workshop on Exploiting Artificial Intelligence Techniques for Data Management, aiDM 2022 - In conjunction with the 2022 ACM SIGMOD/PODS Conference"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85137094838"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-bb9a2673-86b5-4bbb-9f76-fcf7ebe27014"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Micro-architectural analysis of a learned index","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-692960","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Despite their resource- and power-constrained nature, edge devices also exhibit an increase in the available compute and memory resources and heterogeneity, similar to the evolution of server hardware in the past decade. For example, NVIDIA Jetson devices have a system-on-chip (SoC) composed of an ARM CPU and an NVIDIA GPU sharing RAM that could be up to 32 GB. Such an SoC setup offers opportunities to push down complex computations closer to the data source rather than performing them on remote servers. In this paper, we characterize the performance of two types of NVIDIA Jetson devices for end-to-end machine learning pipelines using the TPCx-AI benchmark. Our results demonstrate that the available memory is the main limitation to performance and scaling up machine learning workloads on edge devices. Despite this limitation, some edge devices show promise when comparing against a desktop hardware in terms of power-efficiency and reduction in data movement. In addition, exploiting the available compute parallelism on these devices can benefit not just model training and inference but also data pre-processing. By parallelizing, we get close to an order of magnitude improvement in pre-processing time for one of the TPCx-AI use cases. Finally, while TPCx-AI is a valuable benchmark, it is designed for server settings; therefore, the community needs an end-to-end machine learning benchmark targeting IoT/edge.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Bayer, Robert","Tøttrup, Jon Voigt","Tözün, Pinar"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-695237"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Edge computing","Machine Learning Pipelines","NVIDIA Jetson","System on Chip SoC","TPCx AI Benchmark"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"tpcx ai on nvidia jetsons","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102674781/TPCx_AI_on_Jetsons.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-695237","identifier":[{"id":"85152539475","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-42c2d3b4-0aa7-447f-ad5e-4cde4c809eeb"],"keyword":["Edge Computing","Machine Learning Pipelines","NVIDIA Jetson","System-on-Chip (SoC)","TPCx-AI Benchmark"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B2E0268E176C98ED144059F7200C109D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102674781/TPCx_AI_on_Jetsons.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://itu-dasyalab.github.io/RAD/publication/papers/TPCx_AI_on_Jetsons.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Robert","key":"bayerpau","last":"Bayer","locid":"71115e9d-836f-409e-8c75-27d2de3e9caa","name":"Bayer, Robert","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jon Voigt","key":"tøttruppau","last":"Tøttrup","name":"Tøttrup, Jon Voigt","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pinar","key":"tözünpau","last":"Tözün","locid":"63342b24-28ec-40c5-a7d9-ec49867dc75d","name":"Tözün, Pinar","orcid":"0000-0001-6838-4854","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-29576-8_4","issn":["03029743","16113349"],"issn_org":["03029743"],"journal":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","pages":"49-66","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","volume":"13860"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85152539475"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-42c2d3b4-0aa7-447f-ad5e-4cde4c809eeb"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"TPCx-AI on NVIDIA Jetsons","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-695237","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"The 47th International Conference on Very Large Data- bases (VLDB’21) was held on August 16-20, 2021 as a hybrid conference. It attracted 180 in-person atten- dees in Copenhagen and 840 remote attendees. In this paper, we describe our key decisions as general chairs and program committee chairs and share the lessons we learned.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Bonnet, Philippe","Dong, Luna","Naumann, Felix","Tözün, Pinar"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382945"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Hasso Plattner Institute","Meta"],"keyword":["Conference Organization","Hybrid Conference","In person and Remote Attendance","Lessons learned","VLDB 2021"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"vldb 000000002021 designing a hybrid conference","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86414964/vldb2021.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382945","ids":["itu-70c609f5-dead-4956-a23a-2172df8e9a74"],"keyword":["Conference Organization","Hybrid Conference","In-person and Remote Attendance","Lessons Learned","VLDB 2021"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-2C6EB02D89DE1293AC48804D5A72F4A2"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86414964/vldb2021.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"meta","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Meta","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"hasso plattner institute","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Hasso Plattner Institute","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Philippe","key":"bonnetpau","last":"Bonnet","locid":"19e8d8f1-16b3-457d-b02b-89d9ce850c30","name":"Bonnet, Philippe","orcid":"0000-0002-0234-5374","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Luna","key":"dongpau","last":"Dong","name":"Dong, Luna","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Felix","key":"naumannpau","last":"Naumann","name":"Naumann, Felix","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pinar","key":"tözünpau","last":"Tözün","locid":"63342b24-28ec-40c5-a7d9-ec49867dc75d","name":"Tözün, Pinar","orcid":"0000-0001-6838-4854","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"4","place":"Sigmod record","status":"p","volume":"50"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-70c609f5-dead-4956-a23a-2172df8e9a74"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"VLDB 2021: Designing a Hybrid Conference","type":"Report","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382945","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Micro-architectural behavior of traditional disk-based online transaction processing (OLTP) systems has been investigated extensively over the past couple of decades. Results show that traditional OLTP systems mostly under-utilize the available micro-architectural resources. In-memory OLTP systems, on the other hand, process all the data in main-memory and, therefore, can omit the buffer pool. Furthermore, they usually adopt more lightweight concurrency control mechanisms, cache-conscious data structures, and cleaner codebases since they are usually designed from scratch. Hence, we expect significant differences in micro-architectural behavior when running OLTP on platforms optimized for in-memory processing as opposed to disk-based database systems. In particular, we expect that in-memory systems exploit micro-architectural features such as instruction and data caches significantly better than disk-based systems. This paper sheds light on the micro-architectural behavior of in-memory database systems by analyzing and contrasting it to the behavior of disk-based systems when running OLTP workloads. The results show that, despite all the design changes, in-memory OLTP exhibits very similar micro-architectural behavior to disk-based OLTP: more than half of the execution time goes to memory stalls where instruction cache misses or the long-latency data misses from the last-level cache (LLC) are the dominant factors in the overall execution time. Even though ground-up designed in-memory systems can eliminate the instruction cache misses, the reduction in instruction stalls amplifies the impact of LLC data misses. As a result, only 30% of the CPU cycles are used to retire instructions, and 70% of the CPU cycles are wasted to stalls for both traditional disk-based and new generation in-memory OLTP.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Sirin, Utku","Tözün, Pinar","Porobic, Danica","Yasin, Ahmad","Ailamaki, Anastasia"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383119"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Switzerland","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Intel","Laboratory of Cognitive Neuroscience, EPFL","Oracle Corporation"],"keyword":["In memory OLTP systems","Micro architectural analysis","OLTP","Workload characterization"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"micro architectural analysis of in memory oltp revisited","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86422433/2021_Article_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383119","identifier":[{"id":"85103394583","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-7e80637d-d0a2-408a-9039-5af17fc21145"],"keyword":["In-memory OLTP systems","Micro-architectural analysis","OLTP","Workload characterization"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-49EFC560A61DCAEFFE465FB181B90841"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86422433/2021_Article_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00778-021-00663-8","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ch","key":"laboratory of cognitive neuroscience epfl","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Laboratory of Cognitive Neuroscience, EPFL","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"oracle corporation","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Oracle Corporation","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"intel","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Intel","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Utku","key":"sirinpau","last":"Sirin","name":"Sirin, Utku","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Pinar","key":"tözünpau","last":"Tözün","locid":"63342b24-28ec-40c5-a7d9-ec49867dc75d","name":"Tözün, Pinar","orcid":"0000-0001-6838-4854","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Danica","key":"porobicpau","last":"Porobic","name":"Porobic, Danica","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Ahmad","key":"yasinpau","last":"Yasin","name":"Yasin, Ahmad","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anastasia","key":"ailamakipau","last":"Ailamaki","name":"Ailamaki, Anastasia","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00778-021-00663-8","issn":["0949877X","10668888"],"issn_org":["0949877X"],"issue":"4","journal":"The VLDB Journal","pages":"641-665","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"The VLDB Journal","volume":"30"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85103394583"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7e80637d-d0a2-408a-9039-5af17fc21145"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Micro-architectural analysis of in-memory OLTP: Revisited","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383119","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Open-Channel SSDs are storage devices that let hosts take full control over data placement and I/O scheduling. In recent years, they have gained acceptance in data centers (e.g., Alibaba) and for computational storage (e.g., Pliops). Open-Channel SSDs require a host-based Flash Translation Layer (FTL) that manages the physical address space they expose. Open-source FTLs are now available for Open- Channel SSDs, providing either a generic yet tunable block device interface (e.g., pblk, SPDK, OX-Block), or application- specific FTLs developed for a specific data system (e.g., LightLSM, OX-ELEOS). In this paper, we share our ex- perience developing three of those FTLs in the context of the OX controller. We position Open-Channel SSDs in the SSD landscape and discuss their relevance for data systems. In particular, we argue that Open-Channel SSDs cannot be considered as a uniform class of devices. Our main contri- bution is a description of the key design decisions we took in OX related to Open-Channel SSDs. We reflect on lessons learned and propose hints for the co-design of data systems and Open-Channel SSDs.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Picoli, Ivan Luiz","Hedam, Niclas","Bonnet, Philippe","Tözün, Pinar"],"cuid":["S02-085.36-751867"],"event":{"acronym":"CIDR","end":"2020-01-15","place":"Amsterdam, nl","start":"2020-01-12","title":"10th Annual Conference on Innovative Data Systems Research, CIDR 2020","year":"2020"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Computational storage","Data placement","Flash Translation Layer FTL","I O scheduling","Open Channel SSDs"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"open channel ssd what is it good for","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85096858/cameraready.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-085.36-751867","identifier":[{"id":"85108427461","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ba9409ee-9f36-4f5b-9eb0-1aeb961ebe92","ku-60b63a1e-5977-47d3-b1d6-00de07d7a1e8"],"keyword":["Computational storage","Data placement","Flash Translation Layer (FTL)","I/O scheduling","Open-Channel SSDs"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-2-B48168EA3ED210F06F3DBE3FCFD2A39F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85096858/cameraready.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"http://cidrdb.org/cidr2020/papers/p17-picoli-cidr20.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Programming Languages and Theory of Computation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ivan Luiz","key":"picolipau","last":"Picoli","name":"Picoli, Ivan Luiz","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Niclas","key":"hedampau","last":"Hedam","locid":"c07af2ac-7d8d-482a-ba4c-cd9559612df6","name":"Hedam, Niclas","orcid":"0000-0001-5847-8419","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Philippe","key":"bonnetpau","last":"Bonnet","locid":"19e8d8f1-16b3-457d-b02b-89d9ce850c30","name":"Bonnet, Philippe","orcid":"0000-0002-0234-5374","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pinar","key":"tözünpau","last":"Tözün","locid":"63342b24-28ec-40c5-a7d9-ec49867dc75d","name":"Tözün, Pinar","orcid":"0000-0001-6838-4854","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"place":"Amsterdam, Netherlands","status":"p","title":"Conference on Innovative Data Systems Research"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ba9409ee-9f36-4f5b-9eb0-1aeb961ebe92","ku-60b63a1e-5977-47d3-b1d6-00de07d7a1e8"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Open-Channel SSD (What is it Good For)","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-085.36-751867","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"author_count":2,"contributors":["Tözün, Pinar","Böhm, Alexander"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382066"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"proceedings of the 000000000008th international workshop on testing database systems dbtest sigmod 000000002020 portland oregon june 000000000019 000000002020","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"db","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3395032","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382066","ids":["itu-3638baea-6830-4b2a-a477-d47897fe8a08"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-B536D06891F94BFC90884EFE4EAF97C5"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pinar","key":"tözünped","last":"Tözün","locid":"63342b24-28ec-40c5-a7d9-ec49867dc75d","name":"Tözün, Pinar","orcid":"0000-0001-6838-4854","rank":1,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Alexander","key":"böhmped","last":"Böhm","name":"Böhm, Alexander","rank":2,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3395032","isbn":["9781450380010"],"publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3638baea-6830-4b2a-a477-d47897fe8a08"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Proceedings of the 8th International Workshop on Testing Database Systems, DBTest@SIGMOD 2020, Portland, Oregon, June 19, 2020","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382066","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) has increased in popularity in recent years. The evolution of processor and storage technologies has enabled more advanced ASR mechanisms, fueling the development of virtual assistants such as Amazon Alexa, Apple Siri, Microsoft Cortana, and GoogleHome. The interest in such assistants, in turn, has amplifiedthe novel developments in ASR research. However, despite this popularity, there has not been a detailed training efficiency analysis of modern ASR systems.This mainly stems from: the proprietary nature of many modern applications that depend on ASR; the relatively expensive co-processor hardware that is used to accelerate ASR by big vendors to enable such applications; and the absence of well-established benchmarks. The goal of this paper is to address the latter two of these challenges. The paper first describes an ASR model, based on a deep neural network inspired by recent work, and our experiences building it. Then we evaluate this model on three CPU-GPU co-processor platforms that represent different budget categories. Our results demonstrate that utilizing hardware acceleration yields good results even without high-end equipment. While the most expensive platform (10X price of theleast expensive one) converges to the initial accuracy target10-30% and 60-70% faster than the other two, the differences among the platforms almost disappear at slightly higher accuracy targets. In addition, our results further highlight both the difficulty of evaluating ASR systems due to the complex, long, and resource-intensive nature of the model training in this domain, and the importance of establishing benchmarks for ASR.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Baunsgaard, Sebastian","Wrede, Sebastian Benjamin","Tözün, Pinar"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382894"],"event":{"acronym":"ADMS","title":"International Workshop on Accelerating Analytics and Data Management Systems Using Modern Processor and Storage Architectures"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Benchmarking","CPU GPU co processors","Speech recognition"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"training for speech recognition on coprocessors","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85746756/ADMS20_paper_1_CRC.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382894","ids":["itu-6cff936f-60ef-4d3f-b62c-91d0b517ac71"],"keyword":["Benchmarking","CPU-GPU co-processors","Speech Recognition"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-4269B1A2A68558342D147E37EC50162D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85746756/ADMS20_paper_1_CRC.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"baunsgaardpau","last":"Baunsgaard","name":"Baunsgaard, Sebastian","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian Benjamin","key":"wredepau","last":"Wrede","name":"Wrede, Sebastian Benjamin","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pinar","key":"tözünpau","last":"Tözün","locid":"63342b24-28ec-40c5-a7d9-ec49867dc75d","name":"Tözün, Pinar","orcid":"0000-0001-6838-4854","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"1-10","place":"Tokyo, Japan","status":"p","title":"Twelth International Workshop on Accelerating Analytics and Data Management Systems Using Modern Processor and Storage Architectures"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-6cff936f-60ef-4d3f-b62c-91d0b517ac71"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Training for Speech Recognition on Coprocessors","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382894","year":"2020","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Hash-based authentication is a widespread technique for protecting passwords in many modern software systems including databases. A hashing function is a one-way mathematical function that is used in various security contexts in this domain. In this paper, we revisit three popular hashing algorithms (MD5, SHA-1, and NTLM), that are considered weak or insecure. More specifically, we explore the performance of the hashing algorithms on different hardware platforms, from expensive high-end GPUs found in data centers and high-performance computing centers to relatively cheaper consumer-grade ones found in the homes of end-users. In parallel, we observe the behavior of different hardware platforms. Our results re-emphasize that despite their theoretical strength, the practical utilization of widely used hashing algorithms are highly insecure in many real-world scenarios; i.e., cracking a password of length 6 takes less than 6 seconds using a consumer-grade GPU.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Hedam, Niclas","Mollerup, Jakob","Tözün, Pinar"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383350"],"event":{"acronym":"ADMS","title":"International Workshop on Accelerating Analytics and Data Management Systems Using Modern Processor and Storage Architectures"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Hash based authentication","Hashing algorithms","MD5","NTLM","SHA 1"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"hash based authentication revisited in the age of high performance computers","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85281925/Hash_Based_Authentication_Revisited_in_the_Age_of_High_Performance_Computers.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383350","ids":["itu-8d8fe452-949e-4ecf-8b0d-e672fc939f86"],"keyword":["Hash-based authentication","Hashing algorithms","MD5","NTLM","SHA-1"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-722C55DDD687D6F20B36D68C91858060"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85281925/Hash_Based_Authentication_Revisited_in_the_Age_of_High_Performance_Computers.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"http://www.adms-conf.org/2020-camera-ready/ADMS20_02.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Niclas","key":"hedampau","last":"Hedam","locid":"c07af2ac-7d8d-482a-ba4c-cd9559612df6","name":"Hedam, Niclas","orcid":"0000-0001-5847-8419","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jakob","key":"molleruppau","last":"Mollerup","name":"Mollerup, Jakob","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pinar","key":"tözünpau","last":"Tözün","locid":"63342b24-28ec-40c5-a7d9-ec49867dc75d","name":"Tözün, Pinar","orcid":"0000-0001-6838-4854","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"place":"Tokyo, Japan","status":"p","subtitle":"ADMS’20","title":"International Workshop on Accelerating Analytics and Data Management Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8d8fe452-949e-4ecf-8b0d-e672fc939f86"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Hash-Based Authentication Revisited in the Age of High-Performance Computers","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383350","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"LSM-trees have emerged as the write-optimized index of choice for key-value stores and relational database systems. LSM-trees typically rely on a storage manager on top of a file system for storing data on Solid-State Drives (SSDs). The I/O path thus comprises four layers, each independently managing similar indirection, journaling, and garbage collection mechanisms. Such overhead is increasingly problematic. First, the advent of microsecond-scale SSDs makes it necessary to streamline the I/O software stack. Second, the increasing performance gap between storage and CPU makes it necessary to reduce CPU storage overhead. A solution is to collapse LSM, file system, and SSD management layers into a single software layer embedded on computational storage. Specific commercial solutions are already available. In this short paper, we describe the design space for LSM management on computational storage.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Picoli, Ivan Luiz","Bonnet, Philippe","Tözün, Pinar"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383160"],"event":{"acronym":"DaMoN","title":"International Workshop on Data Management on New Hardware"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["CPU performance","Computational storage","I O path optimization","Key value stores","LSM trees","Microsecond scale SSDs","Relational database systems","Solid State Drives SSDs","Storage management layers","Storage overhead"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"lsm management on computational storage","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84771288/lsm_ocssd.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383160","identifier":[{"id":"85074449516","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-80f976d9-b467-4891-a4ef-2688adbf0eac"],"keyword":["CPU performance","Computational storage","I/O path optimization","Key-value stores","LSM-trees","Microsecond-scale SSDs","Relational database systems","Solid-State Drives (SSDs)","Storage management layers","Storage overhead"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1F84C13369FF5C86DFB52ED294CCB9B5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84771288/lsm_ocssd.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ivan Luiz","key":"picolipau","last":"Picoli","name":"Picoli, Ivan Luiz","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Philippe","key":"bonnetpau","last":"Bonnet","locid":"19e8d8f1-16b3-457d-b02b-89d9ce850c30","name":"Bonnet, Philippe","orcid":"0000-0002-0234-5374","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pinar","key":"tözünpau","last":"Tözün","locid":"63342b24-28ec-40c5-a7d9-ec49867dc75d","name":"Tözün, Pinar","orcid":"0000-0001-6838-4854","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3329785.3329927","isbn":["9781450368018"],"publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 15th International Workshop on Data Management on New Hardware, DaMoN 2019, Amsterdam, The Netherlands, 1 July 2019"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85074449516"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-80f976d9-b467-4891-a4ef-2688adbf0eac"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"LSM Management on Computational Storage","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383160","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"The rising demands of real-time analytics have emphasized the need for Hybrid Transactional and Analytical Processing (HTAP) systems, which can handle both fast transactions and analytics concurrently. Wildfire is such a large-scale HTAP system prototyped at IBM Research - Almaden, with many techniques developed in this project incorporated into the IBM’s HTAP product offering. To support both workloads efficiently, Wildfire organizes data differently across multiple zones, with more recent data in a more transaction-friendly zone and older data in a more analytics-friendly zone. Data evolve from one zone to another, as they age. In fact, many other HTAP systems have also employed the multi-zone design, including SAP HANA, MemSQL, and SnappyData. Providing a unified index on the large volumes of data across multiple zones is crucial to enable fast point queries and range queries, for both transaction processing and real-time analytics. However, due to the scale and evolving nature of the data, this is a highly challenging task. In this paper, we present Umzi, the multi-version and multi-zone LSM-like indexing method in the Wildfire HTAP system. To the best of our knowledge, Umzi is the first indexing method to support evolving data across multiple zones in an HTAP system, providing a consistent and unified indexing view on the data, despite the constantly on-going changes underneath. Umzi employs a flexible index structure that combines hash and sort techniques together to support both equality and range queries. Moreover, it fully exploits the storage hierarchy in a distributed cluster environment (memory, SSD, and distributed shared storage) for index efficiency. Finally, all index maintenance operations in Umzi are designed to be non-blocking and lock-free for queries to achieve maximum concurrency, while only minimum locking overhead is incurred for concurrent index modifications.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Luo, Chen","Tözün, Pinar","Tian, Yuanyuan","Barber, Ronald","Raman, Vijayshankar","Sidle, Richard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384014"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","IBM","IBM Research – Almaden","University of California"],"keyword":["Hybrid Transactional and Analytical Processing","LSM like indexing method","Lock free concurrency control","Multi zone data organization","Unified indexing"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"umzi unified multi zone indexing for large scale htap","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5441/002/edbt.2019.02","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384014","identifier":[{"id":"85064937425","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-bc12d7d8-fe9f-4a48-846b-2ce6770bc46b"],"keyword":["Hybrid Transactional and Analytical Processing","LSM-like indexing method","Lock-free concurrency control","Multi-zone data organization","Unified indexing"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-29D9D350B029057E6272D405B1C86C17"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://openproceedings.org/2019/conf/edbt/EDBT19_paper_6.pdf","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5441/002/edbt.2019.02","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84763035/EDBT19_paper_6.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"university of california","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of California","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"ibm research almaden","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"IBM Research – Almaden","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"ibm","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"IBM","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Chen","key":"luopau","last":"Luo","name":"Luo, Chen","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Pinar","key":"tözünpau","last":"Tözün","locid":"63342b24-28ec-40c5-a7d9-ec49867dc75d","name":"Tözün, Pinar","orcid":"0000-0001-6838-4854","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Yuanyuan","key":"tianpau","last":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yuanyuan","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Ronald","key":"barberpau","last":"Barber","name":"Barber, Ronald","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Vijayshankar","key":"ramanpau","last":"Raman","name":"Raman, Vijayshankar","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Richard","key":"sidlepau","last":"Sidle","name":"Sidle, Richard","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.5441/002/edbt.2019.02","eisbn":["9783893180813"],"pages":"1-12","publisher":"OpenProceedings","status":"p","title":"Advances in Database Technology - 22nd International Conference on Extending Database Technology, EDBT 2019, Lisbon, Portugal, March 26-29, 2019"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85064937425"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-bc12d7d8-fe9f-4a48-846b-2ce6770bc46b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Umzi: Unified Multi-Zone Indexing for Large-Scale HTAP","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384014","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Emerging large-scale real-time analytic applications (real-time inventory/pricing /recommendations, fraud detection, risk analysis, IoT, etc.) require data management systems that can handle fast transactions (OLTP) and analytics (OLAP) simultaneously. Some of them even require analytical queries as part of a transaction. Efficient processing of transactional and analytical requests, however, leads to different design decisions in a system. This article presents the Wildfire system, which targets hybrid transactional and analytical processing (HTAP) for big data. Wildfire leverages Apache Spark to enable large-scale data processing with different types of complex analytical requests and columnar data processing to enable fast transactions and analytics concurrently.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Barber, Ronald","Raman, Vijayshankar","Sidle, Richard","Tian, Yuanyuan","Tözün, Pinar"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383754"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Apache Spark","Big data","Columnar Data Processing","Hybrid Transactional and Analytical Processing HTAP","Real Time Analytics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"wildfire htap for big data","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dbp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105009200803","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383754","identifier":[{"id":"105009200803","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ab490dfd-c806-4e18-b58c-b4c3b68319cd"],"keyword":["Apache Spark","Big Data","Columnar Data Processing","Hybrid Transactional and Analytical Processing (HTAP)","Real-Time Analytics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-91F821F98D7133AB868768C282945BE2"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Ronald","key":"barberpau","last":"Barber","name":"Barber, Ronald","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Vijayshankar","key":"ramanpau","last":"Raman","name":"Raman, Vijayshankar","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Richard","key":"sidlepau","last":"Sidle","name":"Sidle, Richard","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Yuanyuan","key":"tianpau","last":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yuanyuan","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pinar","key":"tözünpau","last":"Tözün","locid":"63342b24-28ec-40c5-a7d9-ec49867dc75d","name":"Tözün, Pinar","orcid":"0000-0001-6838-4854","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-63962-8257-1","eisbn":["9783319639628"],"publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Encyclopedia of Big Data Technologies"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ab490dfd-c806-4e18-b58c-b4c3b68319cd"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Wildfire: HTAP for Big Data","type":"Book Preface, Encycl. Entry","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383754","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Transaction processing has been at the forefront of many influential advancements in the data management ecosystem. In the recent years, many of these advancements have been triggered by the developments in the computer architecture community: multicore parallelism, large main memories, hardware transactional memory, non-volatile memory, remote direct memory access, etc. These developments led to re-thinking of the overall system design for transaction processing systems. In turn, several specialized transaction processing systems have evolved adopting a variety of system designs each with their unique pros/cons. The focus of this article is to survey the techniques proposed in recent years to scale-up transaction processing on modern multicore hardware, and assess how suitable these techniques are in the context of emerging many core hardware and trends in the computer architecture community in general.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Tözün, Pinar"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384507"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Computer architecture trends","Hardware transactional memory","Multicore parallelism","Non volatile memory","Transaction processing"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"hardware assisted transaction processing","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-63962-8178-1","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384507","ids":["itu-dc84f6db-df30-4c3a-aabb-6fa5ce09a736"],"keyword":["Computer architecture trends","Hardware transactional memory","Multicore parallelism","Non-volatile memory","Transaction processing"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7291667E163B720EBEB2B8B5E5EF96BA"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pinar","key":"tözünpau","last":"Tözün","locid":"63342b24-28ec-40c5-a7d9-ec49867dc75d","name":"Tözün, Pinar","orcid":"0000-0001-6838-4854","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-63962-8178-1","eisbn":["9783319639628"],"publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Encyclopedia of Big Data Technologies"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-dc84f6db-df30-4c3a-aabb-6fa5ce09a736"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Hardware-Assisted Transaction Processing","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384507","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Scheduling various data-intensive tasks over the processing units of a server has been a heavily studied but still challenging effort. In order to utilize modern multicore servers well, a good scheduling mechanism has to be conscious of different dimensions of parallelism offered by these servers. This requires being aware of the micro-architectural features of processors, the hardware topology connecting the processing units of a server, and the characteristics of these units as well as the data-intensive tasks. The increasing levels of parallelism and heterogeneity in emerging server hardware amplify these challenges in addition to the increasing variety of data-intensive applications. This article first surveys the existing scheduling mechanisms targeting the utilization of a multicore server with uniform processing units. Then, it revisits them in the context of emerging server hardware composed of many diverse cores and identifies the main challenges. Finally, it concludes with the description of a preliminary framework targeting these challenges. Even though this article focuses on data-intensive applications on a single server, many of the challenges and opportunities identified here are not unique to such a setup, and would be relevant to other complex software systems as well as resource-constrained or large-scale hardware platforms.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Tözün, Pinar","Kotthaus, Helena"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384481"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","TU Dortmund University"],"keyword":["Data Intensive Tasks","Heterogeneous Hardware","Multicore Servers","Parallelism","Scheduling Mechanisms"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"scheduling data intensive tasks on heterogeneous many cores","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84771303/PTdebullMarch.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384481","ids":["itu-da634aae-c0f1-471a-8ba8-3547da2b84ec"],"keyword":["Data-Intensive Tasks","Heterogeneous Hardware","Multicore Servers","Parallelism","Scheduling Mechanisms"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-32C55C2C601704D483577303FFFDCCAB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84771303/PTdebullMarch.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"tu dortmund university","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"TU Dortmund University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pinar","key":"tözünpau","last":"Tözün","locid":"63342b24-28ec-40c5-a7d9-ec49867dc75d","name":"Tözün, Pinar","orcid":"0000-0001-6838-4854","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Helena","key":"kotthauspau","last":"Kotthaus","name":"Kotthaus, Helena","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issue":"1","journal":"IEEE Data Engineering Bulletin","pages":"61-72","status":"p","title":"IEEE Data Engineering Bulletin","title_org":"{IEEE} Data Engineering Bulletin","volume":"42"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-da634aae-c0f1-471a-8ba8-3547da2b84ec"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Scheduling Data-Intensive Tasks on Heterogeneous Many Cores","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384481","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"In a classic transactional distributed database management system (DBMS), write transactions invariably synchronize with a coordinator before final commitment. While enforcing serializability, this model has long been criticized for not satisfying the applications' availability requirements. When entering the era of Internet of Things (IoT), this problem has become more severe, as an increasing number of applications call for the capability of hybrid transactional and analytical processing (HTAP), where aggregation constraints need to be enforced as part of transactions. Current systems work around this by creating escrows, allowing occasional overshoots of constraints, which are handled via compensating application logic. The WiSer DBMS targets consistency with availability, by splitting the database commit into two steps. First, a PROMISE step that corresponds to what humans are used to as commitment, and runs without talking to a coordinator. Second, a SERIALIZE step, that fixes transactions' positions in the serializable order, via a consensus procedure. We achieve this split via a novel data representation that embeds read-sets into transaction deltas, and serialization sequence numbers into table rows. WiSer does no sharding (all nodes can run transactions that modify the entire database), and yet enforces aggregation constraints. Both readwrite conflicts and aggregation constraint violations are resolved lazily in the serialized data. WiSer also covers node joins and departures as database tables, thus simplifying correctness and failure handling. We present the design of WiSer as well as experiments suggesting this approach has promise.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Barber, Ronald","Garcia-Arellano, Christian","Grosman, Ronen","Lohman, Guy","Mohan, C.","Mueller, Rene","Pirahesh, Hamid","Raman, Vijayshankar","Sidle, Richard","Storm, Adam","Tian, Yuanyuan","Tözün, Pinar","Wu, Yingjun"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384169"],"event":{"acronym":"IEEE BigData","title":"IEEE International Conference on Big Data"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Switzerland","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Bern University of Applied Sciences","IBM Analytics","IBM Research – Almaden"],"keyword":["Availability","Hybrid Transactional and Analytical Processing","aggregation constraints","distributed database management system","transactional consistency"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"wiser a highly available htap dbms for iot applications","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85790928/1908.01908.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384169","identifier":[{"id":"85081293846","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-c5e6c3c6-fee2-48eb-86dc-9c4212ad6b4d"],"keyword":["aggregation constraints","availability","distributed database management system","hybrid transactional and analytical processing","transactional consistency"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D1D7BB73CD4AD9546C074AA85BDAD853"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85790928/1908.01908.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1908.01908.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"ibm research almaden","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"IBM Research – Almaden","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"ibm analytics","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"IBM Analytics","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":